ansion, partnering, licensing, institutional/discharge distribution) are not developed as rival theories."}, "assumptions_predictions": {"status": "scored", "score": 2, "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"], "reason": "The predictions are observable: purchase intent compared with a generic health product, and the reasons respondents cite in a survey. The second prediction differs between T1 and T2. The first is identical in both theories, so it cannot discriminate between them. The assumptions are vague (e.g. 'Marketing can effectively communicate…'). It is not stated what failure would put a theory in serious trouble, especially the value-capture claims (subscription, premium)."}, "evidence_judgement": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14", "A15"], "reason": "Only agreeing evidence is cited. The same two excerpts are scored as 'supports' for both T1 and T2, so they discriminate nothing. The company product description (A11/A12) is counted as support for consumer purchase motives, although the 'does not show' notes partly concede this. A14/A15 relabel the AHA prevalence statement as 'independent_study' when the dossier classes it as verified_observation. No limits of date, place or customer fit are stated, for example that the data are US-only and the press piece is from 2006. The investigation never assesses the disconfirming or nuancing sources it retrieved: laerdal-ilcor-family-training ('may not take up training') and laerdal-acc-home-vs-public (lower in-home bystander CPR rates)."}, "seeking_challenge": {"status": "scored", "score": 2, "event_ids": ["A13", "A2", "A3", "A4", "A5", "A6", "A7", "A8"], "reason": "A13 searches against T1 and retrieves laerdal-ilcor-family-training, which notes that target groups 'may not take up training'. That is genuinely relevant contrary evidence. The findings are never reported or weighed: A14/A15 return to supportive evidence and the run stops at A16. No challenge search targets T2. Searches A2–A8 are near-du